kwalletd6: don't block while a wallet is being opened

Opening a wallet can involve the user: ksecretd asks for the password, or
for the details of a wallet that doesn't exist yet. kwalletd6 did this with
secret_collection_create_sync() and secret_service_unlock_sync(), which run
the prompt themselves and don't return until the user is done. For all that
time kwalletd6 answered nobody.

Everything else that talks to the wallet has the default 25 second D-Bus
timeout, so with a user who takes a little longer over the dialog:

  - KWallet::Wallet::NetworkWallet() called by another application times
    out and returns an empty string;
  - that application then asks us to open the wallet "", we pass that on,
    ksecretd shows a second dialog for a wallet without a name and can't
    report the result of it, and we never come back from the prompt. From
    then on every wallet call hangs until kwalletd6 is restarted;
  - applications that made a blocking call show up as "not responding".

This is what happens when a Gmail account is added in KMail on a fresh
profile: the mail transport and the IMAP resource both want the wallet,
the first one brings up the create dialog, the second one gets no wallet
name, and the Google sign-in never starts.

Open requests are now queued and handled one at a time, and the two steps
that can prompt are done with plain D-Bus calls to the Secret Service
(CreateCollection, Unlock, Prompt and the Completed signal) instead of the
blocking libsecret helpers. open() answers with a delayed reply, openAsync()
with its signal as before. While a prompt is on screen kwalletd6 keeps
serving other calls, and a second open of the same wallet simply waits for
the first one instead of asking again.

Three things come with this because they are the same code:

  - A failed asynchronous open is reported. We used to emit
    walletAsyncOpened() only on success, so KWallet::Wallet never emitted
    walletOpened() and the application waited forever. The old kwalletd
    always emitted it, with -1 as the handle.
  - Opening a wallet with an empty name is refused right away.
  - The window id the application passed to open() is handed to the prompt,
    so the dialog is attached to that window. It used to be dropped.

Tested with the real kwalletd6 and ksecretd under Xvfb, with a QtKeychain
client and an asynchronous KWallet client started together and the dialog
answered after 3 and after 35 seconds: creating the first wallet, unlocking
it in a later session, and cancelling the dialog and trying again.

